1
00:00:08,470 --> 00:00:14,380
A class records how long each student slept last night, to the nearest minute.

2
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:19,830
The times go from 5 hours 40 minutes, up to 10 hours 10 minutes.

3
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:23,050
A bar for every possible time would be useless.

4
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:26,480
Almost every bar would have a height of 1.

5
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:29,270
What is the best way to show the pattern?

6
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:32,510
Pause the video and choose.

7
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:39,160
Group the times into classes, like 6 to 7 hours, and count how many are in each class.

8
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:43,200
Remember: discrete data is counted, like the number of pets.

9
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:47,190
Continuous data is measured, like a time or a mass.

10
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:47,900
Your turn.

11
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:50,100
Is mass discrete or continuous?

12
00:00:50,150 --> 00:00:53,420
Pause the video and choose.

13
00:00:53,470 --> 00:00:56,360
Mass is continuous, because it is measured.

14
00:00:56,410 --> 00:01:00,260
Put data into classes with equal class intervals.

15
00:01:00,310 --> 00:01:05,190
For discrete data, groups like 0 to 4, 5 to 9, and 10 to 14 work.

16
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:11,410
For continuous data, use inequalities, so that every value fits exactly one class.

17
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:15,830
6 is less than or equal to t, which is less than 7.

18
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:18,980
For continuous data, draw a frequency diagram.

19
00:01:19,030 --> 00:01:22,740
The bars have no gaps, because the scale is continuous.

20
00:01:22,790 --> 00:01:24,380
Label both axes.

21
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:28,610
For grouped discrete data, draw a bar chart with gaps.

22
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:29,290
Your turn.

23
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:32,120
What do the bars for continuous data have?

24
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:35,340
Pause the video and choose.

25
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:35,890
No gaps.

26
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:37,200
Here is the method.

27
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:41,950
Step 1: choose equal class intervals that cover all the data.

28
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,810
Step 2: tally the data into a frequency table.

29
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:47,370
Step 3: draw the bars.

30
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:51,610
Gaps for discrete data, no gaps for continuous data.

31
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:54,990
Step 4: label the axes, and give a title.

32
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,740
Look at the sleep diagram.

33
00:01:56,790 --> 00:02:00,580
The modal class is the one with the highest frequency, 9.

34
00:02:00,630 --> 00:02:05,100
That is 7 is less than or equal to t, which is less than 8.

35
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:13,980
The number of students is the total of the frequencies: 2 plus 5 plus 9 plus 8 plus 4 plus 2, which is 30.

36
00:02:14,030 --> 00:02:19,900
The fraction who slept at least 9 hours is 4 plus 2, which is 6 out of 30.

37
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:21,730
That is 1 fifth.

38
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:24,920
Masses go from 41 to 78 kilograms.

39
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:31,780
Classes of width 10 could be: 40 to 50, 50 to 60, 60 to 70, and 70 to 80.

40
00:02:31,830 --> 00:02:36,370
They cover all the data, with equal widths.

41
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:44,270
Lee uses classes 140 to 150, 150 to 160, and 160 to 170 centimetres for heights.

42
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:47,680
Which line shows the problem?

43
00:02:47,730 --> 00:02:49,230
The classes overlap.

44
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:52,420
A height of exactly 150 fits two classes.

45
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:54,910
Use inequalities instead.

46
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:00,070
140 is less than or equal to h, which is less than 150.

47
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:03,150
Then 150 to 160, and so on.

48
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:08,910
Health experts recommend that teenagers sleep about 8 to 10 hours a night.

49
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:14,360
In our class, only 14 of the 30 students slept 8 hours or more.

50
00:03:14,410 --> 00:03:15,670
One last question.

51
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:17,930
The frequencies are 4, 9 and 2.

52
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,920
What is the frequency of the modal class?

53
00:03:20,970 --> 00:03:24,290
Pause the video and answer.

54
00:03:24,340 --> 00:03:28,680
The modal class has the highest frequency, which is 9.

55
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:33,160
To remember: use equal class intervals, with no overlaps.

56
00:03:33,210 --> 00:03:40,330
For continuous data, use inequalities, like 6 is less than or equal to t, which is less than 7.

57
00:03:40,380 --> 00:03:43,230
Continuous data has no gaps between bars.

58
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,400
Discrete data has gaps.

59
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:48,700
The modal class has the highest frequency.

60
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:54,150
Great work! Now practise lesson 16.1 on Camday.
